### How does thenApply choose which thread to run on?

The non-async variant runs the callback on the thread that completed the previous stage. If the CF was already completed by the time thenApply is registered, the callback runs on the current (caller) thread.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;

public class ThreadIdentityDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Case 1: CF completes on pool thread, thenApply runs on same thread
        CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> {
            System.out.println("supplyAsync: " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
            return "SAV2001";
        }).thenApply(id -> {
            System.out.println("thenApply: " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
            return id.toUpperCase();
        }).join();

        // Case 2: CF already done, thenApply runs on caller thread
        CompletableFuture<String> done = CompletableFuture.completedFuture("done");
        done.thenApply(s -> {
            System.out.println("thenApply on completed: " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
            return s.toUpperCase();
        }).join();
    }
}
```

```text
supplyAsync: ForkJoinPool.commonPool-worker-1
thenApply: ForkJoinPool.commonPool-worker-1
thenApply on completed: main
```

### Why is blocking in a non-async stage dangerous?

ForkJoinPool.commonPool() has only cores - 1 threads (typically 3 on a 4-core machine). If all threads are blocked in non-async callbacks, no other CompletableFuture can make progress.

```java
// WRONG: blocking IO in non-async callback
for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
    CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> fetchAccountId())
        .thenApply(id -> {
            return callDatabaseBlocking(id); // BLOCKS FJP thread for 200ms!
        });
}
// 3 FJP threads x 200ms: only 15 tasks/sec instead of 500/sec!
// 97 tasks queued, waiting for a free thread.
```

### How do you fix it with thenApplyAsync?

```java
ExecutorService ioPool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(50);

for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
    CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> fetchAccountId())
        .thenApplyAsync(id -> {
            return callDatabaseBlocking(id); // runs on ioPool, not FJP!
        }, ioPool);
}
// FJP threads freed immediately, 50 IO threads handle blocking calls.
```

### How do thenApply and thenApplyAsync compare?

| Feature | thenApply | thenApplyAsync |
|---|---|---|
| Thread | Completing thread or caller | Pool thread (FJP or custom) |
| Thread switch | No (zero overhead) | Yes |
| Good for | parseInt, multiply, format | DB call, HTTP, encryption |
| Danger | Blocking starves completer | None (dedicated thread) |
| Custom pool | N/A | Second parameter |

### Which methods have async variants?

Every non-async chaining method has an async counterpart:

- thenApply / thenApplyAsync
- thenAccept / thenAcceptAsync
- thenRun / thenRunAsync
- thenCompose / thenComposeAsync
- thenCombine / thenCombineAsync
- handle / handleAsync
- whenComplete / whenCompleteAsync
- exceptionally / exceptionallyAsync (Java 12)

### What is the rule for choosing?

- Cheap transform (less than 1ms): thenApply (no overhead).
- Blocking IO (DB, HTTP, file): thenApplyAsync(fn, ioPool).
- CPU-heavy compute (encryption, sorting): thenApplyAsync(fn, cpuPool).
- Never block on ForkJoinPool.commonPool().

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: blocking DB call in non-async
cf.thenApply(id -> jdbcQuery(id)); // blocks FJP thread

// WRONG: async without custom pool for IO
cf.thenApplyAsync(id -> jdbcQuery(id)); // blocks FJP thread (default pool!)

// CORRECT: async with dedicated IO pool
cf.thenApplyAsync(id -> jdbcQuery(id), ioPool); // blocks on IO pool, FJP free
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Does thenApplyAsync guarantee a different thread? Yes, it submits to the executor.
- What is the default executor for async methods? ForkJoinPool.commonPool().
- Can the completing thread be the caller's thread? Yes, if the CF was already completed before the callback was registered.
